<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stirlingshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stirlingshire is a historic county in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> at the head of the Forth estuary. Its control of the lowest crossing of the Forth gave it great strategic importance, and the battles of Stirling Bridge (1297) and Bannockburn (1314) were fought there. In Roman times the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a> crossed its southern part.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stirlingshire.jpg" alt="Stirlingshire" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brythones; derivative work by of Visitor from Wikishire · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stirlingshire_-_Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stirlingshire lies on the Forth-Clyde isthmus, commanding the main overland routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> to central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a> to the north, Clackmannanshire to the east, West Lothian to the south-east, Lanarkshire to the south and Dunbartonshire to the south and south-west. Its sparsely peopled western arm is dominated by Loch Lomond and the Trossachs, with Ben Lomond, at 974 metres, its highest point. The Campsie Fells, Kilsyth Hills and Gargunnock Hills fill the centre, while the flatter east, opening on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>, holds most of the population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1130 King David I made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a>, one of the principal royal strongholds of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, a royal burgh, and the shire itself seems to have been created in his reign, though its origins are obscure. Besides Stirling Bridge and Bannockburn, the county saw King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wallace">William Wallace</a> at the Battle of Falkirk on 22 July 1298, and the Battle of Sauchieburn on 11 June 1488, where rebel nobles nominally led by the 15-year-old Prince James, Duke of Rothesay, fought the followers of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iii-of-scotland">James III of Scotland</a>.</p><p>In 1645 the Covenanter army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-baillie">William Baillie</a> formed near Banton to meet the Royalist forces of Montrose at the Battle of Kilsyth on 15 August, a major battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>. On 17 January 1746 the Jacobites under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a> beat a government army under Lieutenant General Henry Hawley at the Battle of Falkirk Muir. The shire's boundary with Dunbartonshire shifted several times, the parishes of Kirkintilloch and Cumbernauld passing between the two counties in the 13th century, in 1504 and again in 1509.</p><p>Commissioners of Supply governed the shire from 1667, and an elected county council first met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> on 22 May 1890. The reforms of 1889 took Milngavie, Alva and part of Lecropt from the county and gave it Cambuskenneth and parts of Kippen and Logie. The council's coat of arms, granted in 1890, set the red lion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> on the saltire of St Andrew with caltraps and spur-rowels recalling Bannockburn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Stirlingshire was abolished for local government in 1975, most of it passing to the Central region and the area around Kilsyth to Strathclyde. Since 1996 its former area has been divided among the council areas of East Dunbartonshire, Falkirk, North Lanarkshire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a>. Its pre-1975 boundaries survive as a registration county used in land registration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stirlingshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Damnonii appear in Ptolemy's 2nd-century Geography in the region of the Clyde, Ayrshire and parts of <b>Stirlingshire</b>, with places such as Vanduara and Alauna listed among their settlements.</blockquote><a href="/state/damnonii">Damnonii</a></li></ul></section>
